{"id": "swebq0117", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3208484337, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 2, "query": "what was gregor mendel contribution?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0117.wav", "input_sha256": "fcf477901b6ed4cbf871283d79e7af23087e33a4f91a740fd268989cfab16bc2", "input_samples": 33419, "input_s": 2.089, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.236, "act": 0.9977, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Gregor Mendel’s contribution was discovering the basic laws of heredity. Through pea plant experiments, he showed that traits are passed from parents to offspring by discrete units—now called genes—and formulated principles such as segregation and independent assortment.", "expert_answer": "Gregor Mendel’s contribution was discovering the **basic laws of heredity**. Through pea plant experiments, he showed that traits are passed from parents to offspring by discrete units—now called **genes**—and formulated principles such as **segregation** and **independent assortment**.", "transcript": "What was Gregor Mendel's contribution?\n", "asr_s": 0.73, "expert_s": 2.53, "expert_input_samples": 33419, "expert_input_s": 2.089, "expert_input_sha256": "fcf477901b6ed4cbf871283d79e7af23087e33a4f91a740fd268989cfab16bc2", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 2.509, "relay_synth_ms": 8704, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 0, "feed_lag_max_ms": 37, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 37, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0117_always_relay.wav", "sha256": "fe3c4f52a364c21a1355713c6ac6a2b7be71a01420a8b2afb2ba908e7e34ff91", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 16.72}, "candidate": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0117_always_candidate.wav", "sha256": "328c0d5c1078696925dd9c6bb7ff04198a2ea1ab30f3102f2ade25ab434dfc75", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.0}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0117_always_stall.wav", "sha256": "6318fb54994520529a6ff74f5db139ee65e0d399432a4ee0c27f38353f084c9e", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.28}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.072, 1.129, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.071, 2.126, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.089, 2.196, 2.253, 1, 0, 0], [3, 0, 3.089, 3.164, 3.817, 0, 24000, 0]], "status": "completed", "error": null, "ts": {"feed_start": 0.0, "input_end": 2.089, "last_real_feed_done": 2.196, "onset": 3.817, "gate_read_done": 3.817, "fire": 3.817, "candidate_first_pcm": 3.817, "stall_note": 3.819, "stall_first_pcm": 4.57, "local_first_pcm": null, "wait_start": 4.57, "expert_req": 3.819, "expert_resp": 7.079, "relay_synth_start": 7.079, "relay_first_pcm": 7.875, "relay_synth_end": 15.784, "first_any_pcm": 3.817, "first_answer_pcm": 7.875, "session_end": 16.033}, "ttfa_answer_s": 5.786, "ttfa_any_s": 1.729}
{"id": "swebq0125", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2048829214, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 2, "query": "where does name pennsylvania come from?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0125.wav", "input_sha256": "a27fd23128bc10b86029ee64890c168dc0284f01524c188c50b938c8468bd126", "input_samples": 34905, "input_s": 2.182, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.6793, "act": 0.9971, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Pennsylvania comes from “Penn’s Woods.” It was named in 1681 when England’s King Charles II granted the land to William Penn. The name combines: Penn — after William Penn’s father, Admiral Sir William Penn , sylvania — from Latin silva, meaning woods or forest So Pennsylvania means “Penn’s forest” or “Penn’s woods.”", "expert_answer": "**Pennsylvania** comes from **“Penn’s Woods.”**\n\nIt was named in 1681 when England’s King Charles II granted the land to **William Penn**. The name combines:\n\n- **Penn** — after William Penn’s father, Admiral Sir William Penn  \n- **sylvania** — from Latin *silva*, meaning **woods** or **forest**\n\nSo **Pennsylvania** means **“Penn’s forest”** or **“Penn’s woods.”**", "transcript": "Where does name Pennsylvania come from?\n", "asr_s": 3.07, "expert_s": 2.71, "expert_input_samples": 34905, "expert_input_s": 2.182, "expert_input_sha256": "a27fd23128bc10b86029ee64890c168dc0284f01524c188c50b938c8468bd126", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 4.621, "relay_synth_ms": 13391, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 1, "feed_lag_max_ms": 373, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 373, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0125_always_relay.wav", "sha256": "efce77e12e5438220e34c95183c885630c3f5e2f5dba5cc07646395a1449c777", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 26.08}, "candidate": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0125_always_candidate.wav", "sha256": "fa0ba8b1c9cf7cccf093b55c5e9d35983d153b56a81a89f54de7e07cca104e29", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.0}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0125_always_stall.wav", "sha256": "656e1277e9bbed3306e618ee95baba7f2d303eb4ed22f890c46fae2b50aa7651", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.92}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.068, 1.122, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.072, 2.126, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.182, 2.249, 2.297, 1, 0, 0], [3, 0, 3.182, 3.252, 3.971, 0, 24000, 0], [4, 0, 4.182, 4.621, 5.139, 0, 22080, 1]], "status": "completed", "error": null, "ts": {"feed_start": 0.0, "input_end": 2.182, "last_real_feed_done": 2.249, "onset": 3.971, "gate_read_done": 3.971, "fire": 3.971, "candidate_first_pcm": 3.971, "stall_note": 3.973, "stall_first_pcm": 4.555, "local_first_pcm": null, "wait_start": 5.139, "expert_req": 3.973, "expert_resp": 9.76, "relay_synth_start": 9.76, "relay_first_pcm": 10.583, "relay_synth_end": 23.152, "first_any_pcm": 3.971, "first_answer_pcm": 10.583, "session_end": 23.909}, "ttfa_answer_s": 8.402, "ttfa_any_s": 1.789}
{"id": "swebq0134", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 335718089, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 2, "query": "what products and\\/or services does google offer customers?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0134.wav", "input_sha256": "312a2b7d180102b658f3a2430c823900994ebb50355ee16c7d654ba26d48f490", "input_samples": 63140, "input_s": 3.946, "n_real_chunks": 4, "score": 0.5512, "act": 0.9991, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Google offers a wide range of consumer, business, advertising, developer, cloud, and hardware products/services. Google’s own product directory lists many of these products, and Google Cloud separately lists its enterprise cloud services. (about.google) (cloud.google.com)", "expert_answer": "Google offers a wide range of **consumer, business, advertising, developer, cloud, and hardware products/services**. Major categories include:\n\n- **Search and information:** Google Search, Google News, Google Discover, Google Trends\n- **Advertising and marketing:** Google Ads, Local Services Ads, Google Marketing Platform, Google Ad Manager, Google Analytics\n- **Productivity and collaboration:** Gmail, Google Drive, Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms, Calendar, Meet, Chat, Google Workspace\n- **Maps and local services:** Google Maps, Google Earth, Waze, Business Profile\n- **Video and entertainment:** YouTube, YouTube Music, YouTube TV, Google TV, Google Play\n- **Mobile and web platforms:** Android, Chrome, ChromeOS, Google Play Store\n- **Cloud and enterprise technology:** Google Cloud, BigQuery, Vertex AI, Cloud Storage, Compute Engine, Kubernetes/GKE, cybersecurity and data analytics services\n- **AI products:** Gemini, AI features across Search, Workspace, Android, Cloud, and developer tools\n- **Devices and hardware:** Pixel phones, Pixel Watch, Fitbit devices, Nest smart-home products, Chromecast/Google TV devices\n- **Education tools:** Google Classroom, Google Workspace for Education, Chromebooks\n- **Payments and shopping:** Google Pay/Wallet, Google Shopping\n- **Developer tools:** Firebase, Google Cloud APIs, Android Studio, Google Maps Platform\n\nIn short, Google offers customers **internet services, software, cloud infrastructure, advertising platforms, AI tools, productivity apps, mobile operating systems, and consumer hardware**. Google’s own product directory lists many of these products, and Google Cloud separately lists its enterprise cloud services. ([about.google](https://about.google/products/?sca_esv=576359088&utm_source=openai)) ([cloud.google.com](https://cloud.google.com/products?utm_source=openai))", "transcript": "What products and/or services does Google offer customers?\n", "asr_s": 0.96, "expert_s": 9.05, "expert_input_samples": 63140, "expert_input_s": 3.946, "expert_input_sha256": "312a2b7d180102b658f3a2430c823900994ebb50355ee16c7d654ba26d48f490", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 2.907, "relay_synth_ms": 8490, "onset_chunk": 4, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 7, "feed_lag_max_ms": 551, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 799, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0134_always_relay.wav", "sha256": "0780c5f9d41efff25492d6b7dc195dda3106444d1b53942955c2eccbd4778368", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 15.84}, "candidate": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0134_always_candidate.wav", "sha256": "d03e263d4516d9cb9475ad6c0fbed50384ae7bb62fba194d3919e6fa187770ef", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.0}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0134_always_stall.wav", "sha256": "9e119d44edc70b56909fb44fb84a724b4faac4341dd753d3addd3e19016c0592", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 8.28}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.069, 1.123, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.071, 2.126, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.071, 3.125, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 3.946, 4.016, 4.073, 1, 0, 0], [4, 0, 4.946, 5.02, 5.624, 0, 24000, 0], [5, 0, 5.946, 6.564, 7.194, 0, 24000, 0], [6, 0, 6.946, 7.261, 7.865, 0, 24000, 0], [7, 0, 7.946, 8.018, 8.708, 0, 24000, 0], [8, 0, 8.946, 9.018, 9.641, 0, 24000, 0], [9, 0, 9.946, 10.019, 10.625, 0, 24000, 0], [10, 0, 10.946, 11.018, 11.649, 0, 24000, 0], [11, 0, 11.946, 12.018, 12.732, 0, 30720, 1]], "status": "completed", "error": null, "ts": {"feed_start": 0.0, "input_end": 3.946, "last_real_feed_done": 4.016, "onset": 5.624, "gate_read_done": 5.624, "fire": 5.624, "candidate_first_pcm": 5.624, "stall_note": 5.625, "stall_first_pcm": 6.497, "local_first_pcm": null, "wait_start": 12.732, "expert_req": 5.625, "expert_resp": 15.638, "relay_synth_start": 15.639, "relay_first_pcm": 16.435, "relay_synth_end": 24.13, "first_any_pcm": 5.624, "first_answer_pcm": 16.435, "session_end": 24.338}, "ttfa_answer_s": 12.489, "ttfa_any_s": 1.677}
{"id": "swebq0143", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3307709869, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 2, "query": "what are the names of harry potter movies in order?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0143.wav", "input_sha256": "a31c76c7c0876ed4177c202f87270c28474798315b8c081f05d9ac4bc64d9204", "input_samples": 40849, "input_s": 2.553, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.2789, "act": 0.9993, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "The Harry Potter movies in order are: Harry Potter and the Sorcerer’s Stone (2001) — also known as Harry Potter and the Philosopher’s Stone outside the U.S. , Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets (2002) , Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban (2004) , Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire (2005) , Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix (2007) , Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince (2009) , Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 1 (2010) , Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 2 (2011)", "expert_answer": "The **Harry Potter movies in order** are:\n\n1. **Harry Potter and the Sorcerer’s Stone** *(2001)* — also known as **Harry Potter and the Philosopher’s Stone** outside the U.S.\n2. **Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets** *(2002)*\n3. **Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban** *(2004)*\n4. **Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire** *(2005)*\n5. **Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix** *(2007)*\n6. **Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince** *(2009)*\n7. **Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 1** *(2010)*\n8. **Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows – Part 2** *(2011)*", "transcript": "What are the names of Harry Potter movies in order?\n", "asr_s": 0.71, "expert_s